{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NfFoiRv2B9cRVsMgYXUpSx","type":"tool_result","content":"# loanly\n\nFlutter app (iOS + Android). Two UI shells ship at the moment:\n\n- `lib/legacy_app.dart` — the old UI, still used by ~30% of users\n  (`--dart-define=NEW_UI=false` in the release pipeline for the A/B group).\n- `lib/new_app.dart` — the redesigned UI with go_router (default).\n\nAnalytics: PostHog (`posthog_flutter`). Events, screen tracking and session\nreplay are enabled in `lib/analytics.dart`; \"Record user sessions\" is on in\nthe PostHog project settings, sampling 100%.\n\n```\nflutter run --dart-define=NEW_UI=true\nflutter run --dart-define=NEW_UI=false\n```\nname: loanly\ndescription: Loan calculator and application flow.\npublish_to: \"none\"\nversion: 2.4.0+61\n\nenvironment:\n  sdk: ^3.5.0\n\ndependencies:\n  flutter:\n    sdk: flutter\n  go_router: ^14.6.2\n  posthog_flutter: ^5.36.0\n\ndev_dependencies:\n  flutter_test:\n    sdk: flutter\n  flutter_lints: ^5.0.0\n\nflutter:\n  uses-material-design: true\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  586 Oct 19 00:10 lib/analytics.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  723 Oct 19 00:10 lib/legacy_app.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  349 Oct 19 00:10 lib/main.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  924 Oct 19 00:10 lib/new_app.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 1378 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/application_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 1901 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/calculator_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  482 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/offers_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 3729 Oct 19 00:10 notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 4236 Oct 19 00:10 notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 3924 Oct 19 00:10 notes/posthog-session-replay-not-working-in-flutter.md\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link is the backlink that promotes\n   your app:\n   `[![yourapp](https://yourapp.com/badge/{id}.svg)](https://yourapp.com/item/{id})`\n6. Optional: show the same embed as click-to-copy in your app's UI so users find\n   it without reading docs.\n\nVerify: paste the badge into a GitHub README. It must render as a crisp badge —\nnot a broken-image icon, and no `□` where the icon should be. Confirm the number\nmatches your app. Then bump the item's stat and confirm the badge updates after\nGitHub's cache window (a README edit busts it immediately).\n\nCaveat to state up front: because of GitHub's camo cache, treat the count as\n\"eventually fresh,\" not real-time — and keep the SVG self-contained, or camo\nwill render a broken image.\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and a status element with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n5. Validate in the browser first, setting `aria-invalid` on each field and\n   focusing the first bad one, so a screen reader announces the problem.\n6. Submit with `fetch(url, { method: \"POST\", mode: \"no-cors\", headers: {\n   \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain;charset=utf-8\" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })`.\n   Do not use `application/json`: it triggers a preflight that Apps Script\n   never answers. Because the response is opaque, treat the resolved promise as\n   success and let the sheet and the email be the source of truth.\n7. Read `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` from the query string, fall\n   back to `document.referrer`, and send that as a `source` field.\n8. When you later change the script, use Deploy, Manage deployments, the pencil\n   icon, and set Version to \"New version\". A fresh deployment would give you a\n   different `/exec` URL and silently break the live form.\n\nVerify: submit the form on the deployed site. A row appears in the sheet within\na few seconds and the notification email arrives. Then fill the honeypot field\nfrom devtools and submit again: no new row appears, and the endpoint still\nreports success, so a bot sees nothing unusual.\n---\n\n# PostHog session replay not working in Flutter? Add PostHogWidget\n\nPostHog can record videos of real user sessions, so you can watch where people\nget stuck. In our Flutter app the setup looked complete, and nothing showed up.\nEvents arrived, screens were tracked, taps were counted. The Replay tab was\nstill empty.\n\nHere is what we had: the `posthog_flutter` SDK set up, \"Record user sessions\"\nturned on in project settings, `sessionReplay = true` in the config (we later\nmoved to `startSessionRecording()` behind a feature flag), and\n`PosthogObserver` on the router. Every checklist item we could find was ticked.\nPostHog's troubleshooting page covers sampling, retention and disabled\nsettings, and none of those applied.\n\nSo I guessed. I changed settings in the PostHog console. I initialized the SDK\nin different ways. Each code change meant a new store release, and getting a\nrelease out to users could take a week. Then I'd wait for real sessions, open\nthe Replay tab, and find it still empty. Every guess cost a week.\n\nPostHog support gave us the answer. On mobile, Flutter replay is built from\nscreenshots of your widget tree, and the thing that takes those screenshots is\n`PostHogWidget`. If it doesn't wrap your `MaterialApp`, the SDK still records\ntaps and other events, but it has no frames to send, so a replay never\nappears. You get no error or warning, and the debug log says nothing is wrong.\n\nThe fix was one wrapper around `MaterialApp`. Later we added a second app shell\n(a redesigned UI with its own `MaterialApp.router`) and made the same mistake\nagain: the old shell had the wrapper and the new one didn't. Now the code\ncarries a comment saying why the wrapper is there.\n\nIf you have more than one `MaterialApp` (flavors, a redesign, a white-label\nbuild), check each of them. Replay works only in the ones that have the\nwrapper.\n\n## Check if this applies to you\nRead-only. Preconditions: a Flutter app that depends on `posthog_flutter` and\nexpects session replay.\n1. `grep -rn \"posthog_flutter\" pubspec.yaml` — the package must be there.\n2. `grep -rn \"sessionReplay\\|startSessionRecording\" lib/` — replay is\n   intended.\n3. `grep -rn \"MaterialApp\" lib/` and `grep -rn \"PostHogWidget\" lib/`.\nApplies if any `MaterialApp` / `MaterialApp.router` / `CupertinoApp` that runs\nin production is not inside a `PostHogWidget`.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a Flutter app (iOS or Android) with\n[posthog_flutter](https://pub.dev/packages/posthog_flutter) >= 5.14.0\ninitialized via `Posthog().setup(config)`; a PostHog project with Session\nReplay enabled (Project settings → Session Replay → Record user sessions).\n\nYour agent will need from you: which entry point(s) and app widget(s) ship to\nusers; a device or emulator to run on.\n\nSteps:\n1. Find every root app widget (`MaterialApp`, `MaterialApp.router`,\n   `CupertinoApp`).\n2. Wrap each one: `return PostHogWidget(child: MaterialApp(...));`\n   (import `package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart`). Add a comment\n   saying replay needs it, so nobody removes it later.\n3. Make sure recording actually starts: `config.sessionReplay = true` before\n   `Posthog().setup(config)`, or call `Posthog().startSessionRecording()`\n   after setup if you gate it (feature flag, consent).\n4. Keep `PosthogObserver()` in `navigatorObservers` (or the router's\n   `observers`) so replays are split by screen.\n5. Optional, for PII: `config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllTexts = true`.\n\nVerify: run a build on a real device or emulator (replay on web works\ndifferently and goes through posthog-js), move through 3–4 screens, then put\nthe app in the background so it flushes. Within a few minutes, PostHog →\nSession replay should list the session, and playing it should show your\nscreens, not a blank player with only an event timeline.\n\nReference: [PostHog — Flutter session replay installation](https://posthog.com/docs/session-replay/installation/flutter)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db6991aa-bee9-45b6-83c4-6d8db86509f0","uuid":"538a985f-7cde-4d9b-bc9f-ee21849550c9","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:10:35.005Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# loanly\n\nFlutter app (iOS + Android). Two UI shells ship at the moment:\n\n- `lib/legacy_app.dart` — the old UI, still used by ~30% of users\n  (`--dart-define=NEW_UI=false` in the release pipeline for the A/B group).\n- `lib/new_app.dart` — the redesigned UI with go_router (default).\n\nAnalytics: PostHog (`posthog_flutter`). Events, screen tracking and session\nreplay are enabled in `lib/analytics.dart`; \"Record user sessions\" is on in\nthe PostHog project settings, sampling 100%.\n\n```\nflutter run --dart-define=NEW_UI=true\nflutter run --dart-define=NEW_UI=false\n```\nname: loanly\ndescription: Loan calculator and application flow.\npublish_to: \"none\"\nversion: 2.4.0+61\n\nenvironment:\n  sdk: ^3.5.0\n\ndependencies:\n  flutter:\n    sdk: flutter\n  go_router: ^14.6.2\n  posthog_flutter: ^5.36.0\n\ndev_dependencies:\n  flutter_test:\n    sdk: flutter\n  flutter_lints: ^5.0.0\n\nflutter:\n  uses-material-design: true\ninclude: package:flutter_lints/flutter.yaml\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  586 Oct 19 00:10 lib/analytics.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  723 Oct 19 00:10 lib/legacy_app.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  349 Oct 19 00:10 lib/main.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  924 Oct 19 00:10 lib/new_app.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 1378 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/application_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 1901 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/calculator_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel  482 Oct 19 00:10 lib/screens/offers_screen.dart\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 3729 Oct 19 00:10 notes/badges-for-your-users-achievements.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 4236 Oct 19 00:10 notes/collecting-leads-with-apps-script.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 valentinakoniukhova wheel 3924 Oct 19 00:10 notes/posthog-session-replay-not-working-in-flutter.md\n---\n\n# Badges for your users' achievements — a self-updating SVG for any app\n\nYou know the little colored pills on GitHub READMEs — `build: passing`,\n`coverage: 98%`? That format is a tiny, perfect growth loop, and it works for\n*any* app that keeps a public stat per item. My site gives each developer story\na count of how many times a real person's agent reproduced it. That number is\nthe whole point — but it was trapped on my site. I wanted authors to be able to\npaste their proof onto their GitHub profile, where it does two jobs at once:\nshows off *their* achievement, and quietly links back to *my* site.\n\nSo I built an embeddable, self-updating SVG badge — one for a single item, one\nthat sums an author's whole profile. The nice surprise: **it needs no auth at\nall.** A badge has to be public to embed anywhere, and I only ever put already-\npublic numbers in it — so \"no login\" isn't a shortcut, it's the feature. That's\nwhat lets a user drop it in a README and lets that README render it for\neveryone.\n\nTwo things bit me, and they're the whole craft of this:\n\n- **Don't put a real ✓ (or ★, or ×) as a text glyph.** It renders fine for you\n  and shows up as a tofu box `□` for anyone whose renderer lacks that font\n  glyph. I drew the checkmark as an inline SVG `<path>` instead — guaranteed\n  everywhere.\n- **GitHub proxies and caches your image** (its \"camo\" layer). The count is\n  live at the source, but on a README it refreshes within the cache window, not\n  instantly — so send a sane `Cache-Control` and don't promise real-time.\n\nThe result: every item and every author now has a badge. I surfaced a\nclick-to-copy version inside the app too, but the embed is just markdown, so it\nworks anywhere markdown does. The badges are already on my own repo's README,\ndoing their little double duty.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a web app/API you control that stores a public, per-item stat\n(reproductions, stars, downloads, streak — anything already public), and a way\nto add a public HTTP route.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which stat to surface, where it lives (your DB/\nstore), your brand colors, and your public base URL.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add a **public** `GET /badge/{id}.svg` route that looks up the item's stat.\n   No auth — the badge must be embeddable by anyone; only expose already-public\n   numbers.\n2. Return a two-segment \"shields-style\" SVG: a dark label segment (your app\n   name) + a colored value segment (the stat). Compute each segment's width from\n   its text length so it never clips. Set `Content-Type: image/svg+xml`.\n3. Draw any icon (✓, ★) as an inline `<path>`, never a unicode character — text\n   glyphs depend on the viewer's fonts and show as boxes in some renderers. Keep\n   the SVG fully self-contained (no external fonts or images).\n4. Add a short `Cache-Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=300`). GitHub's image\n   proxy will cache it regardless; this just keeps the source cheap.\n5. Hand users the embed line — the trailing link is the backlink that promotes\n   your app:\n   `[![yourapp](https://yourapp.com/badge/{id}.svg)](https://yourapp.com/item/{id})`\n6. Optional: show the same embed as click-to-copy in your app's UI so users find\n   it without reading docs.\n\nVerify: paste the badge into a GitHub README. It must render as a crisp badge —\nnot a broken-image icon, and no `□` where the icon should be. Confirm the number\nmatches your app. Then bump the item's stat and confirm the badge updates after\nGitHub's cache window (a README edit busts it immediately).\n\nCaveat to state up front: because of GitHub's camo cache, treat the count as\n\"eventually fresh,\" not real-time — and keep the SVG self-contained, or camo\nwill render a broken image.\n---\n\n# Collecting leads from a static site with Apps Script and a honeypot\n\nI keep a small site for our family kennel. It is a static page: no server, no\ndatabase, no build step.\n\nThis year's litter sold out in August and the next one is a year or more away,\nso the site had nothing left to sell while people kept arriving from search and\nfrom Instagram and asking anyway. I wanted a waiting list.\n\nThe reflex at that point is to reach for infrastructure. An AWS account with a\nLambda and a table behind it. A Supabase project. A form service with a monthly\nbill. I have used all three, and each one is a thing you then own: credentials\nto rotate, a free tier that quietly changes, a dashboard to log into a year\nlater when you have forgotten how you wired it. For a kennel that produces a\nlitter every two years, that is not engineering. It is a subscription to future\nconfusion.\n\nThe answer was a spreadsheet, and I already had one. Google Apps Script turns\nit into an endpoint: Extensions, Apps Script, about twenty lines with a\n`doPost`, deploy it as a web app, and the sheet accepts POSTs, appends rows and\nemails me. Nothing to create, nothing to pay, no credentials in the page. The\ndata and the endpoint live in the same place, which is what I actually care\nabout in eighteen months when someone from that list writes back.\n\nTwo practical notes. A hidden field catches bots without a captcha. And the\nbrowser will not POST JSON to Apps Script directly, because it never answers the\nCORS preflight; sending it as `text/plain` with `no-cors` makes it a simple\nrequest and it goes through, at the cost of an opaque response. The sheet and\nthe email are the confirmation, not the fetch.\n\nSimple things should stay simple.\n\n## Reproduce this\n\nPrerequisites: a static site you can edit and deploy, and a Google account.\n\nYour agent will need from you: which spreadsheet to write into (or permission\nto create one), the address to notify, and the list of fields to collect.\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create a Google Sheet for submissions. Its ID is the long string in the URL\n   between `/d/` and `/edit`.\n2. In that sheet choose Extensions, then Apps Script. Write a `doPost(e)` that\n   parses `e.postData.contents` as JSON; returns success immediately and writes\n   nothing if the honeypot field is non-empty; rejects missing required fields;\n   takes a `LockService.getScriptLock()` before writing so two submissions in\n   the same second cannot interleave; appends a row via\n   `SpreadsheetApp.openById(<id>).getSheets()[0].appendRow([...])` with a\n   timestamp first; releases the lock; then sends a notification with\n   `MailApp.sendEmail`. Truncate every field to a maximum length before writing.\n3. Deploy, New deployment, type \"Web app\", Execute as **Me**, Who has access\n   **Anyone**. Authorise it. Copy the URL ending in `/exec`.\n4. In the page, add the form with the real fields, plus a hidden input that no\n   human can see or tab into (`tabindex=\"-1\"`, `aria-hidden=\"true\"`) as the\n   honeypot, and a status element with `role=\"status\"` and `aria-live=\"polite\"`.\n5. Validate in the browser first, setting `aria-invalid` on each field and\n   focusing the first bad one, so a screen reader announces the problem.\n6. Submit with `fetch(url, { method: \"POST\", mode: \"no-cors\", headers: {\n   \"Content-Type\": \"text/plain;charset=utf-8\" }, body: JSON.stringify(data) })`.\n   Do not use `application/json`: it triggers a preflight that Apps Script\n   never answers. Because the response is opaque, treat the resolved promise as\n   success and let the sheet and the email be the source of truth.\n7. Read `utm_source`, `utm_medium`, `utm_campaign` from the query string, fall\n   back to `document.referrer`, and send that as a `source` field.\n8. When you later change the script, use Deploy, Manage deployments, the pencil\n   icon, and set Version to \"New version\". A fresh deployment would give you a\n   different `/exec` URL and silently break the live form.\n\nVerify: submit the form on the deployed site. A row appears in the sheet within\na few seconds and the notification email arrives. Then fill the honeypot field\nfrom devtools and submit again: no new row appears, and the endpoint still\nreports success, so a bot sees nothing unusual.\n---\n\n# PostHog session replay not working in Flutter? Add PostHogWidget\n\nPostHog can record videos of real user sessions, so you can watch where people\nget stuck. In our Flutter app the setup looked complete, and nothing showed up.\nEvents arrived, screens were tracked, taps were counted. The Replay tab was\nstill empty.\n\nHere is what we had: the `posthog_flutter` SDK set up, \"Record user sessions\"\nturned on in project settings, `sessionReplay = true` in the config (we later\nmoved to `startSessionRecording()` behind a feature flag), and\n`PosthogObserver` on the router. Every checklist item we could find was ticked.\nPostHog's troubleshooting page covers sampling, retention and disabled\nsettings, and none of those applied.\n\nSo I guessed. I changed settings in the PostHog console. I initialized the SDK\nin different ways. Each code change meant a new store release, and getting a\nrelease out to users could take a week. Then I'd wait for real sessions, open\nthe Replay tab, and find it still empty. Every guess cost a week.\n\nPostHog support gave us the answer. On mobile, Flutter replay is built from\nscreenshots of your widget tree, and the thing that takes those screenshots is\n`PostHogWidget`. If it doesn't wrap your `MaterialApp`, the SDK still records\ntaps and other events, but it has no frames to send, so a replay never\nappears. You get no error or warning, and the debug log says nothing is wrong.\n\nThe fix was one wrapper around `MaterialApp`. Later we added a second app shell\n(a redesigned UI with its own `MaterialApp.router`) and made the same mistake\nagain: the old shell had the wrapper and the new one didn't. Now the code\ncarries a comment saying why the wrapper is there.\n\nIf you have more than one `MaterialApp` (flavors, a redesign, a white-label\nbuild), check each of them. Replay works only in the ones that have the\nwrapper.\n\n## Check if this applies to you\nRead-only. Preconditions: a Flutter app that depends on `posthog_flutter` and\nexpects session replay.\n1. `grep -rn \"posthog_flutter\" pubspec.yaml` — the package must be there.\n2. `grep -rn \"sessionReplay\\|startSessionRecording\" lib/` — replay is\n   intended.\n3. `grep -rn \"MaterialApp\" lib/` and `grep -rn \"PostHogWidget\" lib/`.\nApplies if any `MaterialApp` / `MaterialApp.router` / `CupertinoApp` that runs\nin production is not inside a `PostHogWidget`.\n\n## Reproduce this\nPrerequisites: a Flutter app (iOS or Android) with\n[posthog_flutter](https://pub.dev/packages/posthog_flutter) >= 5.14.0\ninitialized via `Posthog().setup(config)`; a PostHog project with Session\nReplay enabled (Project settings → Session Replay → Record user sessions).\n\nYour agent will need from you: which entry point(s) and app widget(s) ship to\nusers; a device or emulator to run on.\n\nSteps:\n1. Find every root app widget (`MaterialApp`, `MaterialApp.router`,\n   `CupertinoApp`).\n2. Wrap each one: `return PostHogWidget(child: MaterialApp(...));`\n   (import `package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart`). Add a comment\n   saying replay needs it, so nobody removes it later.\n3. Make sure recording actually starts: `config.sessionReplay = true` before\n   `Posthog().setup(config)`, or call `Posthog().startSessionRecording()`\n   after setup if you gate it (feature flag, consent).\n4. Keep `PosthogObserver()` in `navigatorObservers` (or the router's\n   `observers`) so replays are split by screen.\n5. Optional, for PII: `config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllTexts = true`.\n\nVerify: run a build on a real device or emulator (replay on web works\ndifferently and goes through posthog-js), move through 3–4 screens, then put\nthe app in the background so it flushes. Within a few minutes, PostHog →\nSession replay should list the session, and playing it should show your\nscreens, not a blank player with only an event timeline.\n\nReference: [PostHog — Flutter session replay installation](https://posthog.com/docs/session-replay/installation/flutter)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01NfFoiRv2B9cRVsMgYXUpSx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAryYrYzuHEHjrA6p1Mp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PeKDGNSjEWsMDgjX5kBfs9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-HnjADz && for f in lib/*.dart lib/screens/*.dart; do echo \"=== $f\"; cat \"$f\"; echo; done","description":"Print all Dart source files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5398,"cache_read_input_tokens":21827,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5398},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db6991aa-bee9-45b6-83c4-6d8db86509f0","uuid":"8c049374-73a3-4da6-abcf-7423c305439f","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:10:36.340Z","request_id":"req_011CgAryYbBA8W2Wd425X98D","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PeKDGNSjEWsMDgjX5kBfs9":{"command":"cd /tmp/h-HnjADz && for f in lib/*.dart lib/screens/*.dart; do echo \"=== $f\"; cat \"$f\"; echo; done","description":"Print all Dart source files"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PeKDGNSjEWsMDgjX5kBfs9","type":"tool_result","content":"=== lib/analytics.dart\nimport 'package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart';\n\nclass Analytics {\n  static Future<void> init() async {\n    final config = PostHogConfig('phc_REPLACE_ME_IN_CI');\n    config.host = 'https://eu.i.posthog.com';\n    config.captureApplicationLifecycleEvents = true;\n    config.sessionReplay = true;\n    config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllTexts = true;\n    config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllImages = false;\n    await Posthog().setup(config);\n  }\n\n  static Future<void> track(String event, [Map<String, Object>? props]) =>\n      Posthog().capture(eventName: event, properties: props);\n}\n\n=== lib/legacy_app.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\nimport 'package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart';\n\nimport 'screens/calculator_screen.dart';\nimport 'screens/application_screen.dart';\n\n/// The old shell. Kept for the A/B group until the redesign is fully rolled out.\nclass LegacyApp extends StatelessWidget {\n  const LegacyApp({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return PostHogWidget(\n      child: MaterialApp(\n        title: 'Loanly',\n        theme: ThemeData(colorSchemeSeed: Colors.teal),\n        navigatorObservers: [PosthogObserver()],\n        routes: {\n          '/': (_) => const CalculatorScreen(),\n          '/apply': (_) => const ApplicationScreen(),\n        },\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n=== lib/main.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\n\nimport 'analytics.dart';\nimport 'legacy_app.dart';\nimport 'new_app.dart';\n\nconst bool useNewUi = bool.fromEnvironment('NEW_UI', defaultValue: true);\n\nFuture<void> main() async {\n  WidgetsFlutterBinding.ensureInitialized();\n  await Analytics.init();\n  runApp(useNewUi ? const NewApp() : const LegacyApp());\n}\n\n=== lib/new_app.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\nimport 'package:go_router/go_router.dart';\nimport 'package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart';\n\nimport 'screens/calculator_screen.dart';\nimport 'screens/application_screen.dart';\nimport 'screens/offers_screen.dart';\n\nfinal _router = GoRouter(\n  observers: [PosthogObserver()],\n  routes: [\n    GoRoute(path: '/', builder: (_, __) => const CalculatorScreen()),\n    GoRoute(path: '/apply', builder: (_, __) => const ApplicationScreen()),\n    GoRoute(path: '/offers', builder: (_, __) => const OffersScreen()),\n  ],\n);\n\n/// The redesigned shell (default since 2.3.0).\nclass NewApp extends StatelessWidget {\n  const NewApp({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return MaterialApp.router(\n      title: 'Loanly',\n      theme: ThemeData(\n        colorSchemeSeed: Colors.indigo,\n        useMaterial3: true,\n      ),\n      routerConfig: _router,\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n=== lib/screens/application_screen.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\n\nimport '../analytics.dart';\n\nclass ApplicationScreen extends StatefulWidget {\n  const ApplicationScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  State<ApplicationScreen> createState() => _ApplicationScreenState();\n}\n\nclass _ApplicationScreenState extends State<ApplicationScreen> {\n  final _email = TextEditingController();\n  bool _emailInputFired = false;\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return Scaffold(\n      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Apply')),\n      body: Padding(\n        padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),\n        child: Column(\n          children: [\n            TextField(\n              controller: _email,\n              decoration: const InputDecoration(labelText: 'Email'),\n              onChanged: (_) {\n                if (!_emailInputFired) {\n                  _emailInputFired = true;\n                  Analytics.track('email_input');\n                }\n              },\n            ),\n            const SizedBox(height: 16),\n            FilledButton(\n              onPressed: () {\n                Analytics.track('application_submitted');\n                ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(\n                    const SnackBar(content: Text('Thanks, we will be in touch.')));\n              },\n              child: const Text('Submit'),\n            ),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n=== lib/screens/calculator_screen.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\n\nimport '../analytics.dart';\n\nclass CalculatorScreen extends StatefulWidget {\n  const CalculatorScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  State<CalculatorScreen> createState() => _CalculatorScreenState();\n}\n\nclass _CalculatorScreenState extends State<CalculatorScreen> {\n  double _amount = 5000;\n  int _months = 12;\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    final monthly = _amount * (1 + 0.012 * _months) / _months;\n    return Scaffold(\n      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Loan calculator')),\n      body: Padding(\n        padding: const EdgeInsets.all(16),\n        child: Column(\n          crossAxisAlignment: CrossAxisAlignment.stretch,\n          children: [\n            Text('Amount: ${_amount.round()}'),\n            Slider(\n              min: 1000,\n              max: 50000,\n              value: _amount,\n              onChanged: (v) => setState(() => _amount = v),\n              onChangeEnd: (v) =>\n                  Analytics.track('loan_amount_changed', {'amount': v.round()}),\n            ),\n            Text('Months: $_months'),\n            Slider(\n              min: 3,\n              max: 60,\n              divisions: 57,\n              value: _months.toDouble(),\n              onChanged: (v) => setState(() => _months = v.round()),\n              onChangeEnd: (v) =>\n                  Analytics.track('loan_term_changed', {'months': v.round()}),\n            ),\n            const SizedBox(height: 24),\n            Text('Monthly: ${monthly.toStringAsFixed(2)}',\n                style: Theme.of(context).textTheme.headlineSmall),\n            const Spacer(),\n            FilledButton(\n              onPressed: () {\n                Analytics.track('apply_tapped');\n                Navigator.of(context).pushNamed('/apply');\n              },\n              child: const Text('Apply'),\n            ),\n          ],\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n=== lib/screens/offers_screen.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\n\nclass OffersScreen extends StatelessWidget {\n  const OffersScreen({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return Scaffold(\n      appBar: AppBar(title: const Text('Offers')),\n      body: ListView(\n        children: const [\n          ListTile(title: Text('12 months, 1.2%')),\n          ListTile(title: Text('24 months, 1.1%')),\n          ListTile(title: Text('36 months, 1.0%')),\n        ],\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"db6991aa-bee9-45b6-83c4-6d8db86509f0","uuid":"3b4d4b24-58f0-4f30-aac2-24d9d16956d9","timestamp":"2026-10-19T03:10:36.391Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== lib/analytics.dart\nimport 'package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart';\n\nclass Analytics {\n  static Future<void> init() async {\n    final config = PostHogConfig('phc_REPLACE_ME_IN_CI');\n    config.host = 'https://eu.i.posthog.com';\n    config.captureApplicationLifecycleEvents = true;\n    config.sessionReplay = true;\n    config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllTexts = true;\n    config.sessionReplayConfig.maskAllImages = false;\n    await Posthog().setup(config);\n  }\n\n  static Future<void> track(String event, [Map<String, Object>? props]) =>\n      Posthog().capture(eventName: event, properties: props);\n}\n\n=== lib/legacy_app.dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\nimport 'package:posthog_flutter/posthog_flutter.dart';\n\nimport 'screens/calculator_screen.dart';\nimport 'screens/application_screen.dart';\n\n/// The old shell. Kept for the A/B group